    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The two parts of every recursive function"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**The base case** — an input small enough to answer directly, with no further call. Here: `factorial(1)` is 1. Without a base case the calls never stop.",
        "**The recursive case** — express the answer using the same function on a **smaller** input, then finish the job. Here: `factorial(n) = n × factorial(n − 1)`. Each call must move closer to the base case."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "call-stack",
      "caption": "Each call waits for the one it made; the answers come back in reverse order."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The code"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function factorial(n) {\n  if (n <= 1) return 1;            // base case: no more calls\n  return n * factorial(n - 1);     // pauses here until the inner call returns\n}\n\nfactorial(4);"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def factorial(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 1                  # base case: no more calls\n    return n * factorial(n - 1)   # pauses here until the inner call returns"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "long factorial(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;            // base case: no more calls\n    return n * factorial(n - 1);     // pauses here until the inner call returns\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "long long factorial(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;            // base case: no more calls\n    return n * factorial(n - 1);     // pauses here until the inner call returns\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n)** — one call per number. **Space O(n)** — the calls wait on top of each other until the base case answers (the next lesson shows exactly where that memory goes)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "How to write a recursive function"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        "**Say what the function returns** for any input, in one sentence: \"`sumDigits(n)` returns the sum of n's digits.\"",
        "**Find the base case**: the smallest input you can answer outright. A single digit is its own sum.",
        "**Shrink the problem**: how does the answer for `n` follow from the answer for something smaller? The digits of 1234 are the digits of 123, plus 4.",
        "**Trust the recursive call** — the \"leap of faith\". Assume `sumDigits(123)` already works; don't trace it in your head. If the base case is right and every call gets smaller, it works."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function sumDigits(n) {\n  if (n < 10) return n;                          // one digit: it is its own sum\n  return sumDigits(Math.floor(n / 10)) + (n % 10);   // the other digits, plus the last one\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def sum_digits(n):\n    if n < 10:\n        return n                          # one digit: it is its own sum\n    return sum_digits(n // 10) + n % 10   # the other digits, plus the last one"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int sumDigits(int n) {\n    if (n < 10) return n;                     // one digit: it is its own sum\n    return sumDigits(n / 10) + n % 10;        // the other digits, plus the last one\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int sumDigits(int n) {\n    if (n < 10) return n;                     // one digit: it is its own sum\n    return sumDigits(n / 10) + n % 10;        // the other digits, plus the last one\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Why use recursion?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Every recursive function can be written as a loop, and loops are usually a little faster. But many problems are **naturally recursive** — trees, nested brackets, \"try every way\" searches, divide-and-conquer sorts — and the recursive code is shorter and closer to how you'd describe the solution. Backtracking, tree traversals, merge sort, quick sort and most dynamic programming start as recursion."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Common mistakes"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**No base case, or one that's never reached** — `factorial(-1)` with a base case of `n == 1` calls forever, until the program crashes with a stack overflow.",
        "**Not shrinking** — calling `f(n)` from `f(n)` again, or shrinking by the wrong amount and jumping past the base case.",
        "**Forgetting to return** — writing `factorial(n - 1)` without using its result.",
        "**Tracing every call in your head** — for deep recursion it's hopeless. Check the base case and one step instead."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Key takeaways"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "Recursion = a **base case** answered directly + a **recursive case** that uses the same function on a smaller input.",
        "Each call waits for the call it made; answers come back in reverse order.",
        "Write it by stating what the function returns, then trusting the smaller call.",
        "It's the foundation of backtracking, tree algorithms and dynamic programming."
      ]
    }
